#!/usr/bin/env python3
from pathlib import Path
Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The test ensures that creating a modular route component (Blueprint) with a name containing dot characters raises a ValueError to preserve Flask\'s hierarchical endpoint naming convention used for nested blueprints.\\n\\nThe validation occurs in the `Blueprint.__init__` method in `src/flask/sansio/blueprints.py` at lines 198-199:\\n\\n```python\\nif \\".\\" in name:\\n    raise ValueError(\\"\'name\' may not contain a dot \'.\' character.\\")\\n```\\n\\nFlask uses dots (`.`) as delimiters to construct hierarchical endpoint names when blueprints are nested. When a blueprint registers routes, the endpoint name is constructed using the pattern `\\"{name_prefix}.{blueprint_name}.{endpoint}\\"` (see `BlueprintSetupState.add_url_rule` at line 112 in `src/flask/sansio/blueprints.py`). When blueprints are nested, the parent blueprint\'s name becomes the `name_prefix` for child blueprints (line 376: `bp_options[\\"name_prefix\\"] = name`).\\n\\nFor example, when a blueprint named \\"bp\\" contains a nested blueprint registered with name \\"sub\\", the resulting endpoint becomes \\"bp.sub.index2\\" (as demonstrated in `test_blueprint_renaming` at lines 1043-1044 of `tests/test_blueprints.py`).\\n\\nIf blueprint names themselves could contain dots (e.g., \\"app.ui\\"), Flask would be unable to distinguish between:\\n- A blueprint name that contains a dot (e.g., \\"app.ui\\")\\n- A nested blueprint hierarchy (e.g., a blueprint \\"app\\" containing a blueprint \\"ui\\")\\n\\nThis would break the endpoint naming convention and make it impossible to correctly resolve endpoint names for nested blueprints. The constraint ensures that dots are reserved exclusively as delimiters in the hierarchical naming system, maintaining the integrity of Flask\'s blueprint endpoint resolution mechanism."}' + '\n')
